1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:05,240
The Allocation Scandal. fifty-five million people die annually.

2
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:11,880
The National Institutes of Health has an annual budget of approximately FORTY-SEVEN BILLION DOLLARS.

3
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:16,640
You would think those two facts would be related. They are not.

4
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:25,840
Of two hundred and forty-seven billion dollars the N.I.H. spent over a decade, exactly three point three percent went to

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,440
testing whether treatments actually work in humans. The other ninety-six point seven percent went to everything else.

6
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:42,320
That might be defensible if we'd already tested all the known safe compounds that are piled up waiting for human testing.

7
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:51,740
We haven't. There are roughly nine thousand five hundred compounds with established safety profiles, and ninety-nine point

8
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:56,680
seven percent of their possible disease applications have never been tested.

9
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:02,340
The pipeline is overflowing with candidates. The bottleneck is trials.

10
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:11,080
It's like spending ninety-six point seven percent of your grocery budget on cookbooks and three point three percent on food,

11
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:15,340
then wondering why you're starving. This isn't just inefficiency.

12
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:24,120
It's a body count. A J.A.M.A. study broke down two hundred and forty-seven point three billion dollars in N.I.H.

13
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:30,460
spending (twenty ten to twenty nineteen). Each of these categories funds valuable work.

14
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:37,540
The question is whether the ratio makes sense when thousands of safe compounds sit untested.

15
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:45,840
Eighty-four point nine percent of the budget, or two hundred and nine point nine billion dollars, went to basic research

16
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,780
like understanding disease mechanisms, molecular biology, and genomics.

17
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:59,440
This is essential for identifying new targets, but eighty-five cents of every dollar is a lot of understanding and not much

18
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:08,020
testing. Eleven point eight percent, totaling twenty-nine point three billion dollars, was spent on non-trial applied research

19
00:02:08,539 --> 00:02:14,600
which translates discoveries toward practical use without the final step of testing them in patients.

20
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:24,120
The clinical and translational science awards, or C.T.S.A. infrastructure program, uses about one billion dollars per year

21
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:31,880
for trial infrastructure, coordination, and training. Observational studies for population health data collection, such

22
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:39,800
as the All of Us program at two point one six billion dollars, are valuable for identifying patterns, but observation without

23
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:48,660
intervention doesn't cure anyone. Other applied research includes drug characterization, health services research, and epidemiology.

24
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:57,560
Only three point three percent, or eight point one billion dollars, went to phased clinical trials, which is the only category

25
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:02,240
that directly tests whether treatments work in humans (phases one to three).

26
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:09,460
Phase one received one point five billion dollars, which is eighteen point five percent of the clinical trial budget.

27
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:15,240
Phase two received three point five billion dollars, or forty-three point two percent.

28
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:23,300
Phase three received two point six billion dollars, or thirty-two point one percent, while the N.I.H.

29
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:31,060
covers only three point seven to four point three percent of phase three costs, leaving completion to industry, which only

30
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:38,580
funds trials with patent-protected profit potential. About five hundred million dollars, or six point two percent, went

31
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:46,760
to other unclassified costs for cross-phase overhead, pilot studies, and trials not classified to a single phase.

32
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:54,240
Pragmatic trials, which can be thirty times cheaper, remain severely underfunded despite proven efficiency.

33
00:03:55,460 --> 00:04:00,780
Why This Allocation Exists. This isn't bureaucratic accident.

34
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:09,560
It's lobbied outcome. Drug companies love the N.I.H. Taxpayers absorb the risk of basic research; industry patents whatever

35
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:15,740
works. It's like if your neighbor paid for your kitchen renovation, and then you charged them rent to eat there.

36
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:20,390
But independent trials comparing drugs head-to-head? Those are terrifying.

37
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:25,740
They might reveal that the eighty thousand dollar drug works about as well as the four dollar generic.

38
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:31,460
PhRMA, the pharmaceutical lobby, has consistently opposed comparative effectiveness research.

39
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:39,940
When Congress created the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute, or PCORI, in twenty ten, industry successfully lobbied

40
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:44,100
to prohibit it from making coverage recommendations based on cost-effectiveness.

41
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:49,800
Congress literally passed a law saying "don't fund research that might lower drug prices."

42
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:56,380
They wrote it down. In legislation. On purpose. The revolving door helps.

43
00:04:56,380 --> 00:04:59,480
N.I.H. advisory committees include industry representatives.

44
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:05,980
Former N.I.H. officials join pharmaceutical boards. Academic researchers depend on industry grants.

45
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:13,640
Everyone's incentives align against cheap, fast trials testing whether expensive drugs outperform generics.

46
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:22,720
It's not a conspiracy. It's just that everyone involved happens to benefit from the same terrible outcome, purely by coincidence,

47
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,020
repeatedly, for decades. Basic research produces publications.

48
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:34,440
Publications produce tenure. Tenure produces more grants. Nobody in this cycle needs anyone to get better.

49
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:39,760
They need papers. The three point three percent allocation isn't a mystery.

50
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:45,060
It's a business model. The Efficiency Gap: A Tale of Two Trials.

51
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:51,010
The difference between efficient and inefficient trial design isn't theoretical.

52
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:54,980
Humanity accidentally ran a controlled experiment on itself during COVID.

53
00:05:55,660 --> 00:05:59,830
Two countries. Same disease. One spent like it was trying to cure death.

54
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:05,360
The other actually did. RECOVER Initiative (N.I.H. Approach).

55
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:13,220
Budget: one point six six five billion dollars (one point one five billion dollars plus five hundred and fifteen million

56
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:18,360
dollars in twenty twenty-four) (source). Timeline: four years and counting.

57
00:06:19,160 --> 00:06:24,240
Patients enrolled: approximately thirty thousand. Trials completed: Zero.

58
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,980
Cost per patient: fifty-five thousand five hundred dollars.

59
00:06:29,620 --> 00:06:34,240
RECOVERY Trial (U.K. Approach). Budget: twenty million dollars.

60
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:39,600
Timeline: six months. Patients enrolled: forty-eight thousand.

61
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:44,700
Treatments found: Multiple, including dexamethasone (saved over one million lives).

62
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:53,560
Cost per patient: five hundred dollars. The N.I.H. spent one hundred and eleven times more per patient and achieved infinitely

63
00:06:53,560 --> 00:07:00,740
less (dividing by zero trials completed produces infinity, which is fitting because that's how long patients waited).

64
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:09,160
This efficiency gap is not unique to RECOVERY. A systematic review of sixty-four pragmatic trials found a median cost of

65
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:14,900
ninety-seven dollars per patient. The British cured disease for the price of a nice dinner per patient.

66
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:19,440
America spent the price of a luxury SUV per patient and cured nothing.

67
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:25,600
The problem isn't that curing disease is hard. The N.I.H. doesn't have a capability problem.

68
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:31,500
It has a shopping addiction. The Death Toll: Opportunity Cost of Misallocation.

69
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:39,920
When you misallocate the vast majority of a forty-seven billion dollar annual budget into low-efficiency research instead

70
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:44,190
of high-efficiency clinical trials, you aren't just wasting money.

71
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:50,000
You are composting people. Cost Per Q.A.L.Y. (Quality-Adjusted Life Year).

72
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:55,020
Standard N.I.H. Portfolio: fifty thousand dollars per Q.A.L.Y.

73
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:58,400
at standard medical research R.O.I. (and that's being generous).

74
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:08,240
Pragmatic Platform Trials: four dollars per Q.A.L.Y. Every one dollar spent on efficient pragmatic trials buys twelve thousand

75
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:12,720
five hundred times more health than a dollar spent on the current N.I.H.

76
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:21,720
portfolio. The ADAPTABLE trial proved pragmatic trials cost thirty times less than traditional R.C.T.s (fourteen million

77
00:08:21,720 --> 00:08:26,140
dollars versus four hundred and twenty million dollars). Same science.

78
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:31,880
Fewer catered meetings. The "Death Equivalent" of Budget Misallocation.

79
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:40,679
The annual opportunity cost is approximately one hundred million quality-adjusted life years, or Q.A.L.Y.s, and about seven

80
00:08:40,679 --> 00:08:49,240
million death-equivalents per year. Even the most conservative estimate is approximately ten million Q.A.L.Y.s and seven

81
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:57,820
hundred thousand preventable deaths annually. For every dollar spent on pragmatic trials producing Q.A.L.Y.s, the N.I.H.

82
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:02,400
spends twelve thousand five hundred dollars to achieve the same result.

83
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:10,720
You are operating at zero point zero zero eight percent of your potential capacity to save lives.

84
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:18,360
If your car ran at zero point zero zero eight percent efficiency, it would move one inch per gallon.

85
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:25,720
If your heart pumped at zero point zero zero eight percent efficiency, you'd be dead before finishing this sentence.

86
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:34,000
Your medical research system runs at zero point zero zero eight percent efficiency, and you gave it a raise.

87
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:41,660
The Translation Crisis: All Theory, No Medicine. The problem is not a shortage of ideas.

88
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:48,360
It is a shortage of anyone bothering to test them. There are over twenty thousand F.D.A.

89
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:56,060
approved drugs known to be safe in humans. There are between forty-seven hundred and sixty-eight hundred repurposable compounds,

90
00:09:56,740 --> 00:10:04,300
which are clinically-tested candidates (including three thousand four hundred and twenty-two marketed drugs) gathering dust.

91
00:10:05,340 --> 00:10:12,000
There are hundreds of plausible dementia interventions. The number of clinical trials available for your grandma in Saint

92
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:19,760
Louis is zero. You have a warehouse of safe molecules that have never been systematically tested for new uses.

93
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:24,360
The warehouse is full. The testing facility is closed for a team-building retreat.

94
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:34,560
The N.I.H. is, by its own metrics, a publishing house that occasionally dabbles in medicine: Two point five million N.I.H.

95
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:39,520
funded papers are published annually. About fifty new treatments are approved annually.

96
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:45,440
The conversion rate from paper to patient is zero point zero zero two percent.

97
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:55,080
For every fifty thousand papers, one drug reaches a human. The rest become citations in other papers, which become citations

98
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:59,900
in other papers, in an infinite loop of academics reading each other's homework.

99
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:07,800
Plenty of Knowledge. No Translation. About five percent of patients have participated in trials.

100
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:11,880
About fifty percent of patients are willing to participate if invited.

101
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:16,340
Only nine to eleven percent of patients are actually invited.

102
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:21,960
Half of all patients would join a clinical trial if asked. Almost nobody asks.

103
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:28,420
It's like having a room full of volunteers and a clipboard, and choosing to study the clipboard.

104
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:33,720
Understanding disease and curing disease are completely different activities.

105
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:40,740
One requires microscopes. The other requires giving people medicine and seeing if they stop dying.

106
00:11:41,540 --> 00:11:47,160
The N.I.H. chose microscopes. The Public Goods versus Club Goods Scam.

107
00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:53,140
The N.I.H. claims to produce "public goods" like open science and training.

108
00:11:53,600 --> 00:12:01,020
What it actually produces is a free R. and D. department for pharmaceutical companies, funded by the same people who then

109
00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:08,400
pay full price for the drugs. Taxpayers funded research behind ninety-nine percent of new drugs (three hundred and fifty-six

110
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,840
drugs, twenty ten to twenty nineteen). Industry patents the winners.

111
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:20,240
The public pays again through high drug prices. When research leads to a dead end, the public eats the loss.

112
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:26,780
You pay for the lottery ticket. They cash the winnings. The only things the N.I.H.

113
00:12:26,780 --> 00:12:35,300
*should* fund are things private companies *cannot* profitably fund: Open pragmatic trial platforms: thirty times cheaper

114
00:12:35,300 --> 00:12:39,440
than traditional trials, yet receive minimal N.I.H. funding.

115
00:12:40,220 --> 00:12:48,300
Re-purposing generic drugs: more than four thousand seven hundred clinically-tested compounds where no patent equals no

116
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:57,140
profit equals no interest. Comparative effectiveness data: Head-to-head trials revealing which drugs actually work best

117
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:02,220
(pharma's nightmare). These are the *only* things the N.I.H.

118
00:13:02,220 --> 00:13:08,900
should be funding. Instead, the N.I.H. explicitly avoids them to "not crowd out industry."

119
00:13:09,990 --> 00:13:16,580
Translation: "We won't run the trials that would lower drug prices, because the people who set drug prices asked us nicely.".

120
00:13:17,460 --> 00:13:21,500
The Patient Disconnect: Zero Correlation with Health Outcomes.

121
00:13:22,140 --> 00:13:29,180
Correlation between N.I.H. funding priorities and actual disease burden: zero point zero seven.

122
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:36,520
That is not a typo. On a scale of zero (random) to one (perfect), the N.I.H.

123
00:13:36,640 --> 00:13:42,680
scores "basically a coin flip." A dartboard would allocate research funding more rationally.

124
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:48,860
What patients want. Medicine that works. Access to experimental therapies.

125
00:13:49,440 --> 00:13:55,220
Trials they can join. A cure before they die. What N.I.H. funds.

126
00:13:55,240 --> 00:14:00,780
Molecular mechanisms. Papers in prestigious journals. Research empires.

127
00:14:01,040 --> 00:14:05,560
Institutional overhead. Patients don't control the money. Committees do.

128
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:10,400
And committees optimize for the continued existence of committees.

129
00:14:10,860 --> 00:14:15,660
This is true of all committees, everywhere, since the invention of committees.

130
00:14:16,400 --> 00:14:20,380
Nobody has ever convened a committee that recommended fewer committees.

131
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:28,920
The Track Record. Since nineteen seventy, the N.I.H. has spent over one point one trillion dollars (inflation-adjusted).

132
00:14:29,900 --> 00:14:37,500
Diseases eradicated: Zero. This isn't because eradicating disease is impossible: The W.H.O.

133
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:45,560
eradicated smallpox for three hundred million dollars. Jonas Salk developed the polio vaccine in a university lab and gave

134
00:14:45,560 --> 00:14:50,640
it away for free (which is why nobody at the N.I.H. talks about him at parties).

135
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:56,140
Veterinarians have eradicated multiple animal diseases while the N.I.H.

136
00:14:56,140 --> 00:15:02,580
was forming subcommittees about forming subcommittees. Cows have better healthcare outcomes than you do.

137
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:08,380
What Would Actually Work. The U.K. RECOVERY trial already proved it.

138
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:13,780
five hundred dollars per patient instead of forty-one thousand dollars.

139
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:19,540
First life-saving result in under one hundred days. Over one million lives saved.

140
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:25,260
The blueprint exists. It is not theoretical. It happened. In England.

141
00:15:25,800 --> 00:15:30,080
During a pandemic. While Americans were arguing about horse dewormer.

142
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:35,820
The recipe: one. Pay for results: Treatment equals payment.

143
00:15:35,920 --> 00:15:44,860
No treatment equals no payment. Radical, I know. two. Publish everything: Including failures (especially failures).

144
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:50,500
three. Eliminate gatekeepers: Let patients vote with participation.

145
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:58,500
four. Automate administration: Smart contracts instead of committees (committees cannot be trusted; see above).
